-- title:
--   Finiteness of the full-level field over the q-scaled Γ₀(qM) field
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, let $M\ge 1$ be a natural number with $q \nmid M$, and work inside the Laurent series field $\bar{\mathbb Q}((t))$ over $K=\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\;\mathbb Q$. Let $F_{\mathbb Q}(\Gamma_0(qM)) =$ `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (Gamma0 (q * M))` be the subfield of $\mathbb Q((t))$ generated over $\mathbb Q$ by all quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_f/\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_g$ coming from two modular forms $f,g$ of a common weight $k$ for $\Gamma_0(qM)$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ and $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,p_g\ne 0$, and let its base change `laurentBaseChange K` be the subfield of $K((t))$ generated over $K$ by the coefficientwise images of that field. Apply the ring homomorphism `qExpand K q`, which multiplies all exponents by $q$ (that is, $f(t)\mapsto f(t^{q})$), to this base-changed field, and let $A$ be the intermediate field of $K((t))/K$ generated by the resulting set. Let $\bar F =$ `fieldBar q M` be the base change to $K$ of the field `xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M) (levelH q M)`, where `levelH q M` is the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb Z/q^2M)^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$. The assertion is that `relfinrank`, the degree of $\bar F$ over $A \cap \bar F$, is non-zero, i.e. that this relative extension is finite.
--
--   This is the finiteness half of the degree estimate comparing the full-level $q$-expansion field at level $q^2M$ with the $q$-scaled function field of $\Gamma_0(qM)$; combined with the inclusion $A \le \bar F$ supplied by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.qExpand_coe_mem_fieldBar_of_mem`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.qExpand_coe_mem_fieldBar_of_mem), it says that $\bar F$ is a finite extension of $A$. It feeds the statements [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.adjoin_qExpand_image_le_fieldBar_and_relfinrank_pos_and_le`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.adjoin_qExpand_image_le_fieldBar_and_relfinrank_pos_and_le) and its variants for $q = 2$ and $q = 3$, which package the inclusion together with upper and lower bounds for the relative degree.
